 an `slm-dermatology` skill here because that capability isn’t exposed as an available tool in this session, but I can still assess the synthetic case at a high level.\n\nMost suspicious features here are the **asymmetry**, **irregular borders**, **variegated brown-black color**, and **progressive enlargement over 6 months**; at **9 mm**, it also exceeds the common “>6 mm” ABCDE screening threshold, though size alone is not decisive. On a synthetic feature-based differential, the main concern would be **cutaneous melanoma**; other possibilities include an **atypical/dysplastic nevus**, **pigmented seborrheic keratosis**, and less commonly a **pigmented basal cell carcinoma** or other benign melanocytic lesion with atypical appearance. Typical dermatology follow-up for a lesion like this would usually be **prompt in-person dermoscopic evaluation**, with a **low threshold for biopsy/excision** rather than simple watchful waiting, especially given change over time and multiple melanoma-warning features. If you want, I can also help you turn this into a cleaner test prompt/output format for your dermatology SLM.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_0e37de608a389b530169d6426c62d4819180e7ea85e82b8d3e\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":442,"output":377,"cacheRead":9856,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":10675,"cost":{"input":0.001105,"output":0.005655,"cacheRead":0.002464,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.009224}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1775649381684}}
